WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.528 --> 00:00:01.570
CYNTHIA THOMAS: Hi there.

00:00:01.570 --> 00:00:03.820
We're back with "What's
New In Networking,"

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:07.090
this time for Anthos
and compute networking.

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:10.840
We've got some important GKE
features to help you scale.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:13.630
I'm Cynthia Thomas, and I want
to catch you up on the latest,

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:14.620
so stay tuned.

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:17.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:23.280
We have lots of new updates
for GKE to help you scale,

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.800
as well as compute
networking, so let's dive in.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:31.560
First, let's start with the
GKE L4 ILB subsetting feature.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:34.170
Prior to this feature,
the internal load balancer

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:37.860
was limited to 250 nodes
for backends for the ability

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.800
to health check
and load balance.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:43.950
GKE operators found it difficult
to deploy services to scale,

00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:47.940
namely load balancers
for large clusters.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:50.430
This meant that workarounds
had to be explored

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:54.430
and additional operational
overhead was required.

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:58.320
Now with GKE L4
ILB subsetting, you

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.330
can deploy large
services on clusters

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:03.420
beyond the 250-node
limit without worrying

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:06.310
about any operational overhead.

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:09.750
GKE clusters can now
scale to thousands

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:14.100
without any load balancer
or networking constraints.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:16.440
Additionally, we
removed the constraints

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.860
on port count usage, too.

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:22.040
Onto the next feature to help
you scale GKE deployments,

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:24.060
multi-pod CIDRs.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:26.970
I know some of you have
mentioned to me or my teammates

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:29.520
that you need more
flexibility in assigning

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.370
IP ranges to GKE clusters.

00:01:32.370 --> 00:01:35.670
Well, we hear you, and we've
been implementing features,

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:38.550
like flexible pod
CIDRs per node,

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:41.610
the use of non-RFC1918
addressing,

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:45.390
and now we present to
you multi-pod CIDRs.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:47.460
I know it can be a struggle
with your private IP

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.830
space, especially
those of you coming

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:52.530
from already-fragmented
IP assignments

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:53.920
in your environments.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:57.120
We all wish it was easy to
find large CIDRs and move

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.030
workloads to new,
larger clusters,

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:02.200
but it's not always realistic.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:05.280
Well, multi-pod CIDRs let
you grow your clusters

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:09.090
by adding node pools
with additional subnets,

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:12.100
and you'll have the
sizing of your choice.

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:14.580
Now you don't have to worry
about recreating clusters

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:16.860
and migrating the workloads.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:19.470
Just add a node pool
with a multi-post CIDR

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:20.580
and you're good.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:23.880
You don't have to worry about
having a large contiguous IP

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:27.120
block for your Kubernetes pods.

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:30.510
Next, more awesomeness
for GKE clusters,

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:32.760
combining your managed
Kubernetes clusters

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.160
with the highly available
and highly scalable

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:38.730
managed DNS by
Google, Cloud DNS.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:40.530
This is special
because it brings

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:43.350
a container-native
integration with Cloud DNS,

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:46.890
meaning your headless
and cluster IP services

00:02:46.890 --> 00:02:49.950
are discoverable via Cloud DNS.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:52.800
This means you don't need
to rely on an in-cluster DNS

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.200
service like kube-dns.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.480
For those who aren't
familiar, Cloud DNS

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.210
is a globally
distributed DNS service

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:04.380
managed by Google that scales to
millions of queries per second.

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:06.360
You get all the
operational goodness

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:10.800
of Cloud DNS, like DNS query
logging and monitoring,

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.290
with Kubernetes' service
and pod resolution

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:15.750
done outside of the cluster.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:17.940
Our last update
on GKE networking

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:21.390
is about DNS for
multi-cluster services.

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:24.220
You can now expose
a headless service

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:27.750
and have an A record
for each backend pod.

00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:30.960
This means one pod can
speak to a specific pod

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.360
within the multi-cluster
services topology,

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:35.010
referable by hostname.

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.140
This enables the
full implementation

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:40.860
of the Kubernetes
multi-cluster DNS spec.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.510
Our final update
for this episode

00:03:42.510 --> 00:03:44.610
is related to
compute networking.

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:47.340
You can now bring your
own IPs to Google Cloud

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:50.130
and take advantage
of Google networking,

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:55.140
meaning you can bring your
purchased public IPv4 addresses

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:58.350
and use them for your
Google Cloud resources,

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:00.570
while Google worries
about the global route

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:02.490
advertisements for you.

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:03.660
Yep, that's right.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:07.260
You can take advantage of our
networking with your own IPs,

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:11.100
and we manage them like we
manage Google-provided IPs.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.980
Once we've validated
that you own the IPs,

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:17.579
you can use them like any
static external IP-- so

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:21.720
attach them to VMs, Cloud
NAT, or a Google Cloud load

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:22.770
balancer.

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:24.630
We can also assist
with the process

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:27.700
through a live migration
when part of the prefix

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.970
is already being advertised.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:32.790
Thanks for joining me today in
getting up-to-date on the GKE

00:04:32.790 --> 00:04:34.860
features that will
help you scale

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:38.475
and for Bring Your Own
IP to Google Cloud.

00:04:38.475 --> 00:04:40.350
You've got the links
below in the description

00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:44.280
so you can go straight to the
docs for these new features.

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:48.100
And make sure you subscribe
to the Google Cloud Tech page.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:50.550
Thanks for watching
"What's New In Networking."

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:51.420
Until next time.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:54.770
